      "created_at": "2025-12-30T20:21:46Z",
      "updated_at": "2025-12-31T07:26:49Z",
      "body": "I was able to reproduce the current behavior using a minimal pytester-based test.\n\nWhen python_files = *.py is set, assertion rewriting is applied not only to collected test modules, but also to non-test, locally imported modules. \n\nI added a small repro where an external module (extmod.py) is imported by a test, and its assertion is rewritten. The failure output shows a rewritten. The failure output shows a rewritten assertion message (assert 10 == 42) instead of a bare AssertionError, even though the module itself is not a collected test. \n\nThe test intentionally does not rely on '+where' introspection, as assertion output formatting can vary depending on the expressions. It only documents that assertion rewriting occurs for a non-test module imported by a test. \n\nThis behavior appears to come from _should_rewrite relying primarily on python_files patterns, without distinguishing between collected test files and other imported modules on the import path. \n\nI have added a passing test that documents this current behavior. Happy to adjust expectations or explore design options if this is considered unintended.",
      "author_association": "NONE",
